# =============================================================================
# GridPro fig6 REPRODUCED: a C/H topology with an H OUTER FIELD -- 160 blocks x
# opening downstream onto a RECTANGULAR far field. 24 blocks.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch.dat
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case: it lives beside
# docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method.md (section 13) rather than in config/,
# nothing in examples/ or config/ refers to it, and no gate covers it.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Section 12a read GridPro's own fig6 and found its single-body
# far field is not a radial fan against a circle: the lines wrap the nose in a
# C and then run PARALLEL AND STREAMWISE, the wake leaves the trailing edge as
# a straight band, and the outer boundary is a RECTANGLE. Section 12b put a
# rectangle on the O-ring family and measured it much WORSE, because a ring is
# a ring. Section 12e said what it would take instead -- this file -- and this
# is it.
#
# It does NOT supersede gridpro_rings.dat beside it. That one is the O-ring
# family sections 9-11 measure against GridPro's own tutorial circle; this one
# is the arrangement its FIGURES show. Section 13e measures the difference: the
# C/H is better on mean non-orthogonality and on size grading, the O-ring on
# the worst corner and on holding the far-field spacing.
#
# THE DOMAIN IS 9 x 6 CHORDS, and that is a COMPARISON choice, not the right
# size to solve on. It is closest to what the O-ring cases of sections 9-11
# have -- GridPro's tutorial circle of radius 3 -- so section 13e's mesh
# comparison is made at a comparable domain. Section 14c measured what it costs
# the SOLVE: at Re 200 a 9 x 6 box cuts through the viscous region and its
# residual band's median sits 1.5x above the one at the shipped C-grid case's
# own 30 x 20 (1.44e-04 against 9.34e-05 over 10000 iterations).
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py --domain -10 20 10
#
# is that one, and section 13e carries both meshes' numbers.
#
# GENERATED BY
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py \
#       --layers 3 --split 16 --turn-blend 0.8 --out gridpro_ch_h.json
#
# WHY THOSE THREE NUMBERS, each measured rather than chosen (section 17):
#   --layers 3    the rings. They sit at a CONSTANT DISTANCE from the body
#                 (0.05 and 0.25 chords), not at a constant fraction of each
#                 radial -- the radials run 2.0 chords at the nose to 6.0 down
#                 the wake, so a fraction makes a band three times thicker at
#                 one end. And each layer's first cell is the previous layer's
#                 LAST, which is what makes the ring seams invisible: without
#                 it the radial cells are 0.002 / 0.013 / 0.17 and the seams
#                 are 6x and 14x jumps, worst area ratio 13.7 against 3.5.
#   --split 16    32 sectors. The nose needs stations, and section 16 showed
#                 that getting them by CLUSTERING makes the smallest cell tiny
#                 and stalls the solve. Getting them by COUNT does not.
#   --turn-blend 0.8   with 32 sectors this keeps the smallest cell at
#                 1.63e-06, above section 16c's threshold, so the solve
#                 converges.
#
# GATE 2: 10000 iterations, exit 0, no NaN, and the residual DESCENDS to
# 6.5e-06 -- unlike the single-layer case beside it, which stalls at 9.0e-05.
#   ./run_pipeline.sh docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_fig6_demo.json
#
# It does NOT supersede gridpro_ch.dat: that one is what sections 13-16 measure,
# and every figure there is quoted at its settings.
# =============================================================================

MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_h.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- the rectangle is declared, corner by corner.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/naca0012_cgrid.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing: the far-field and outlet edges
# carry no binding, so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar. A real case would want
# inlet / outlet / farfield told apart, which needs a far-field geometry file.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family (section 8g); five is
# where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_ch_h/mesh.vtk
